# libgcrypt -- GnuPG's crypto primitives.
#
# Needed by gnupg, which is needed by gpgme, which flatpak takes as a hard
# dependency with no option guard. Nothing else in Duct uses it: openssl covers
# TLS for curl and python, and this is here purely because the GPG signature
# verification behind every flatpak install runs through gnupg.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/libgcrypt/libgcrypt-1.11.2.tar.bz2
SRC_SHA256=6ba59dd192270e8c1d22ddb41a07d95dcdbc1f0fb02d03c4b54b235814330aac
SRC_DIR=libgcrypt-1.11.2

# --disable-doc, off to save build time rather than because it would fail:
# texinfo is packaged and the documentation would build.
#
# --disable-static RESTATES A DEFAULT and is kept for the same two reasons as the
# rest of the chain -- consistency with every library recipe here, and because
# the default belongs to libtool rather than to this package, so a flag that
# restates it today does not restate it permanently.
#
# THE LONG LIST OF CPU-FEATURE OPTIONS IS DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED, and that is
# the decision worth recording in this recipe.
#
# configure offers --disable-aesni-support, --disable-avx-support,
# --disable-avx2-support, --disable-shaext-support, --disable-pclmul-support,
# --disable-sse41-support, --disable-drng-support, --disable-padlock-support and
# more. All are ENABLED BY DEFAULT, and the instinct on reading them -- that
# enabling instruction-set extensions produces a binary that crashes on hardware
# without them -- is wrong for this library. libgcrypt compiles each accelerated
# implementation into a separate object and SELECTS AT RUN TIME from CPU feature
# detection, which is why the options exist for debugging and for reproducibility
# work rather than for portability.
#
# Disabling them would therefore not make the package safer; it would make every
# AES and SHA operation slower on every machine that has the instructions, on a
# distribution that has to verify a GPG signature for every flatpak install and
# every package it downloads. Named here because "disable the CPU-specific thing
# so it runs everywhere" is a very reasonable-sounding change for someone to make
# later, and it is the wrong one.
#
# --disable-asm is the same shape one level up and is also left alone: it turns
# off the assembler MPI and cipher cores entirely, which is a large slowdown for
# no portability gain on the two architectures this distribution targets.
#
# --disable-jent-support is left alone too. The Jitter entropy collector is
# enabled by default and is an additional entropy source; removing entropy
# sources from the library that generates keys is not a tidy-up.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static --disable-doc"
